WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.208
From summit crater to sea cliff.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.169
Queen Mary's Peak holds a small crater lake and is snow-capped in winter.

00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:13.547
Below the central cone is a dissected plateau the islanders call the Base,

00:00:13.547 --> 00:00:14.730
cut by deep gulches.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.374
Sea cliffs hundreds of metres high ring most of the island.

00:00:19.374 --> 00:00:24.239
Only narrow coastal plains, built by lava flows and debris fans, offer flat land.

00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:29.285
The plain on the north-west coast is the only one large enough for a village and its

00:00:29.285 --> 00:00:30.186
potato patches.

00:00:30.186 --> 00:00:33.129
And in 1961, a new vent opened right at its edge.

00:00:34.229 --> 00:00:36.966
From the sea you can see how little flat land there is:

00:00:36.966 --> 00:00:39.802
the village is squeezed between the cliffs and the shore.

00:00:40.902 --> 00:00:46.323
On the seafloor around the island, lobe-shaped deposits record past flank collapses:

00:00:46.323 --> 00:00:49.679
giant landslides, of a kind that can cause tsunamis.
